BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:camargo-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090443Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Camargo Fellowship 2027-2028 for Artists\, Scholars and T
 hinkers (Cassis\, France)
DESCRIPTION:The Camargo Foundation invites artists\, scholars and thinkers 
 to its flagship Core Fellowship: a ten-week self-directed residency on its
  clifftop campus overlooking the Mediterranean in Cassis\, France\, dedica
 ted to research\, experimentation and interdisciplinary exchange. Curators
  apply under the Thinkers category\, which covers accomplished professiona
 ls and practitioners in cultural and creative fields who are professionall
 y engaged in critical thought\, including curators\, critics and journalis
 ts\; projects should engage theoretical\, artistic or societal questions. 
 WHAT YOU GET: a stipend of EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 total)\, basic tra
 nsportation funding\, and a furnished apartment with an equipped kitchen o
 n campus. Fourteen fellowships are awarded per year: seven for artists and
  seven for scholars and thinkers. RESIDENCY PERIODS: Fall 2027 (8 Septembe
 r to 17 November 2027) or Spring 2028 (23 February to 4 May 2028). ELIGIBI
 LITY: artists in visual arts\, performance\, writing\, film\, music or mul
 tidisciplinary work with at least 5 years of professional practice\; schol
 ars holding or pursuing a PhD focused on French and Francophone cultures o
 r cross-cultural Mediterranean studies\; and thinkers as described above. 
 APPLICATION: via Submittable with a 100-word proposal summary\, a 1\,200-w
 ord proposal narrative\, a 300-word rationale for Camargo\, a 300-word car
 eer-stage rationale\, a CV (max 3 pages)\, work samples and two references
 . NOTE: the foundation has announced that this is the final Fellowship sel
 ected through a public open call until further notice\; a new selection pr
 ocess begins in July 2027\, making this the last chance to apply directly.
 \n\nOrganisation: Camargo Foundation\nLocation: Camargo Foundation campus\
 , Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.\nAward: EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 to
 tal for the ten-week residency)\, plus basic travel funding and a furnishe
 d apartment with equipped kitchen on the Camargo campus. 14 fellowships pe
 r year: 7 for artists and 7 for scholars and thinkers. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Ten weeks\, either 8 September to 17 November 2027 or 23 Febru
 ary to 4 May 2028.\n\nMore: https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fello
 wship-2026-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Camargo Foundation campus\, Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bow-seat-true-blue-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090443Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: True Blue Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:Bow Seat's True Blue Fellowship funds youth-led projects that u
 se the creative arts as their primary method for building awareness or dri
 ving action on ocean\, waterways and climate change issues\, including env
 ironmental justice. Twelve fellows are selected each year and receive up t
 o USD 2\,500 in project funding alongside mentorship\, capacity-building p
 rogramming\, and access to a peer community that includes the Future Blue 
 Youth Council and Bow Seat staff. ELIGIBILITY: youth worldwide aged 16 to 
 24 at the start of the fellowship period in January. Individuals\, groups 
 and youth-led organisations may apply\, with one designated project leader
  for a group. Projects must be new or emerging\, meaning less than two yea
 rs old\, and should span at least one year running January to December. Ap
 plications must be submitted in English\, though translation tools are acc
 eptable\, and applicants should be comfortable with spoken English for pee
 r collaboration. NOTE: this is a youth programme with an upper age limit o
 f 24\, and the creative-arts requirement is central rather than incidental
 \, so proposals built on other methods are out of scope. Deadline 30 Augus
 t 2026 at 11:59pm ET.\n\nOrganisation: Bow Seat Ocean Awareness Programs\n
 Location: Remote\, open to youth worldwide.\nAward: Up to USD 2\,500 in pr
 oject funding per fellow\, plus mentorship and capacity-building programmi
 ng. Twelve youth are selected annually. No application fee.\nDuration: One
  year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https://bowseat.org/programs/tru
 e-blue-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://bowseat.org/programs/true-blue-fellowship/?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to youth worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mycelia-fellowship-social-resonance-lab-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261025T090443Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mycelia Fellowship 2026 (Social Resonance Lab)
DESCRIPTION:The inaugural Mycelia Fellowship by Social Resonance Lab\, a co
 llaborative research studio exploring how ethical storytelling fosters hum
 an connection\, selects two practitioners advancing innovative projects at
  the intersection of ethical storytelling\, arts\, civic engagement\, AI\,
  or the digital landscape. TWO TRACKS: Arts as a Civic Tool (artistic prac
 tice for public engagement and social issues) and AI and the Digital Lands
 cape (technology's influence on narratives and collective memory). Fellows
  receive a USD 2\,000 stipend\, direct advising and consultancy from the S
 RL team\, a published piece on the SRL Substack\, a guest spot on an upcom
 ing ethical storytelling podcast\, and co-lead a workshop presenting their
  work to practitioners and the public. The fellowship strengthens\, docume
 nts\, refines or scales EXISTING projects\; do not pitch a completely new 
 idea\, and connect your work clearly to ethical storytelling and one of th
 e two tracks. ELIGIBILITY: US residents only (including territories\, must
  be able to file a W-9 and use a US bank account) with an existing project
  underway\, intersectional practice awareness\, and strong writing and res
 earch skills. Remote\, 8 weeks\, November to December 2026. No application
  fee. Deadline: 13 September 2026\, 11:59 PM.\n\nOrganisation: Social Reso
 nance Lab\nLocation: Remote\, US residents only (including territories)\nA
 ward: USD 2\,000 stipend per fellow\; two fellows selected for the inaugur
 al cohort\nDuration: 8-week remote fellowship\, 1 November to 31 December 
 2026\n\nMore: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdj_qxRZmRZcNdVcQvX
 z9mZIhZTj-Iau-cZVb-kowZtRER6Dw/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdj_qxRZmRZcNdVcQvXz9mZIhZTj-I
 au-cZVb-kowZtRER6Dw/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, US residents only (including territories)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gias-gulbenkian-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090443Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261015
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:Deadline: Gulbenkian Institute for Advanced Study Fellowships 2027/
 28 (Lisbon)
DESCRIPTION:Residential think-tank fellowships at the Calouste Gulbenkian F
 oundation in Lisbon\, explicitly open to artists as well as academics (aff
 iliated or independent)\, emeritus professors and professionals outside ac
 ademia. Fellows spend 3 to 8 months pursuing self-directed work in any dom
 ain\, provided it needs no laboratory or experimental infrastructure and i
 s not predominantly applied\; selection weighs the CV at 40 percent and th
 e innovativeness of the proposal at 60 percent. WHAT YOU GET: travel\, tra
 vel and health insurance\, a monthly stipend and a partial housing subsidy
 \, with fellow status continuing after the stay. ELIGIBILITY: open interna
 tionally\; past GIAS fellows must wait five years. Applications via the My
 Gulbenkian platform with CV\, a short description of proposed activities a
 nd two referee names. DEADLINE: the annual window runs 1 July to 15 Octobe
 r\; this round closes 15 October 2026 for 2027/28 stays.\n\nOrganisation: 
 Gulbenkian Institute for Advanced Study (GIAS)\, Calouste Gulbenkian Found
 ation\nLocation: Lisbon\, Portugal. Open internationally\; residential fel
 lowships at the Gulbenkian in Lisbon.\nAward: Up to 15 fellowships a year.
  Each covers travel costs\, travel and health insurance\, a monthly stipen
 d and a partial housing subsidy\; the stipend level is set at selection ba
 sed on CV and length of stay. No application fee.\nDuration: 3 to 8 months
  during the 2027/28 academic year\n\nMore: https://gulbenkian.pt/gias/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://gulbenkian.pt/gias/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Lisbon\, Portugal. Open internationally\; residential fellowships 
 at the Gulbenkian in Lisbon.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mensch-maschine-visit-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090443Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261202
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mensch Maschine VISIT Residency Fellowship 2027: Energy G
 hosts (E-WERK Luckenwalde / Akademie der Künste)
DESCRIPTION:The Mensch Maschine VISIT Residency Program is an annual fellow
 ship offering four fully funded fellowships to artists from around the wor
 ld\, run by the JUNGE AKADEMIE of the Akademie der Künste (Berlin) with V
 ISIT\, the artist-in-residence programme of the E.ON Foundation\, E-WERK L
 uckenwalde and Ujazdowski Castle Centre for Contemporary Art (Warsaw). The
  2027 edition particularly encourages projects at the intersection of art\
 , energy and ecology exploring the theme ENERGY GHOSTS: the unseen dimensi
 ons of energy approached through mythology\, cosmology or speculative fict
 ion\, the invisible infrastructures\, histories and residues that haunt co
 ntemporary life\, and energy as an ecological\, political or spiritual for
 ce. Proposals are welcome from visual art\, performance\, film\, installat
 ion\, sound\, writing and new media. WHAT YOU GET: EUR 20\,000 per fellow 
 for artist fees and production costs of a new project (research and develo
 pment\, travel\, material and presentation costs)\; three fellows receive 
 studio space and accommodation at E-WERK Luckenwalde (a former power stati
 on about 40 minutes south of Berlin)\, one is accommodated by the Akademie
  der Künste in Berlin. The three-month residency runs roughly September t
 o November 2027 with on-site presence of at least 75% required\, a retreat
  at Ujazdowski Castle in Warsaw in September 2027\, events across the thre
 e venues\, and a closing symposium in October 2027 at the Akademie der Kü
 nste where fellows show work in progress alongside the 2026 cohort. JURY: 
 Nolan Oswald Dennis\; Chus Martinez (Institute Art Gender Nature\, TBA21)\
 ; Sarah Johanna Theurer (Fondation Beyeler)\; Anh-Linh Ngo (Akademie der K
 ünste)\; and Tiara Roxanne. APPLICATION: via the VISIT portal at visit-en
 ergy.com/bewerben. DEADLINE: 1 December 2026\, 23:59 CET.\n\nOrganisation:
  JUNGE AKADEMIE of the Akademie der Künste\, VISIT (E.ON Foundation)\, E-
 WERK Luckenwalde and Ujazdowski Castle Centre for Contemporary Art\nLocati
 on: E-WERK Luckenwalde\, Brandenburg\, Germany (3 fellows) and Akademie de
 r Künste\, Berlin (1 fellow)\, with a retreat at Ujazdowski Castle CCA in
  Warsaw\, Poland. Open to artists worldwide.\nAward: Four fully funded fel
 lowships of EUR 20\,000 each\, intended for artist fees and production cos
 ts of a new project (research and development\, travel\, materials and pre
 sentation costs)\, plus studio space and accommodation for the three-month
  residency.\nDuration: Three months\, roughly September to November 2027 (
 the AdK notice gives mid-August to mid-November)\; fellows must live and w
 ork on site at least 75% of the time.\n\nMore: https://adk.de/news/2026/10
 /open-call-mensch-maschine-stipendium?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://adk.de/news/2026/10/open-call-mensch-maschine-stipendium?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:E-WERK Luckenwalde\, Brandenburg\, Germany (3 fellows) and Akademi
 e der Künste\, Berlin (1 fellow)\, with a retreat at Ujazdowski Castle CC
 A in Warsaw\, Poland. Open to artists worldwide.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
